
[A] -- (Add Port)

Add  a  new port to the port list, which will be used for dialing out.  You
will  then  be  prompted for the fields of the port.  Note that there is no
validity  checking, so entering wrong data will cause unpredictable results
(e.g.,  entering "narrator.device" for the device name).  When you are done
with entering the port data enter "Y" at the prompt to accept the port.

The special codes for modem commands are:

~  - wait 1/2 a second
\r - send a carriage return (ASCII 13)
     This will be required as the last characters for most modem strings
     (e.g., "ATZ\r").
\n - send a new line (ASCII 10)
\b - send a backspace character (ASCII 8)

The fields are:

Serial Device
-------------
 Device to use when opening the port.

Device Unit
-----------
 Unit number to use when opening the port.

Baud Rate
---------
 Baud rate of outdialing modem.

Data Length
-----------
 Length  (in  bits)  of  each datum sending or receiving through the serial
port.

Parity
------
 The kind of parity check to perform on data transfered.

Stopbits
--------
 The number of stop bits.

Handshaking
-----------
 The type of handshaking to perform.

High Speed Mode
---------------
 Whether  to  open  the serial port in high speed ("RADBOOGIE") or not.  In
high speed mode, certain error checking is not performed.

Serial Port Shared Mode
-----------------------
 Determine  whether  other  comm  programs  should  be allowed to share our
serial port.

DTR Hangup
----------
 If true, close and re-open the serial port (toggles the DTR (Data Terminal
Ready)  signal  line)  to  drop carrier, else send the hangup string to the
modem.   Note  that  older  version of the A2232 (multi-serial card) driver
does not handle DTR drop.

Check Carrier Before Sending Modem Commands
-------------------------------------------
 Check if a carrier is detected before sending a modem command.

Modem Initialize String
-----------------------
 String to send to the modem whenever the serial port is opened.

Exit String
-----------
 String to send to the modem whenever the serial port is closed.

Dial Prefix
-----------
 The  string  to send to the modem to tell it to dial.  This string will be
sent before the number to be dial is sent.

Dial Suffix
-----------
 The string to send to the modem after a phone number is sent.

Hangup String
-------------
 The string to send to hangup the modem.

Connect String
--------------
 The  string  to  look for to determine whether the modem is connected to a
system.

Disconnect String
-----------------
 The  string  to  look  for  to  determine  whether  the modem is no longer
connected to the phone.

Busy String
-----------
 The string to look for when the system to dial is busy.

No Dialtone String
------------------
 The  string  to look for when the modem attempts to dial, but is unable to
because the phone company is not listening.

Error String
------------
 The string to look for when sending a command to the modem causes an error.

Voice String
------------
 The  string  to  look for when a connection is made to voice system (e.g.,
answering machine, or someone picked up the phone).

Ring String
-----------
 The  string to look for when someone is calling in and causes the modem to
ring.

Dial Timeout
------------
 The number of seconds to wait until a connection is made.  If a connection
is not made within this period of time, the dialing will be aborted.

Redial Delay
-------------
 The number of seconds to wait between each dial attempt.

